      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point on the line passing through \\( (2,2,1) \\) and \\( (5,1,-2) \\) with an \\( x \\)-coordinate of 4, we start by determining the parametric equations of the line. The direction vector of the line is given by the difference between the coordinates of the two points:\n\n\\[\n\\vec{d} = (5-2, 1-2, -2-1) = (3, -1, -3)\n\\]\n\nThe parametric equations of the line can be written as:\n\n\\[\nx = 2 + 3t\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 2 - t\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3t\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the \\( x \\)-coordinate of the point is 4. Substituting \\( x = 4 \\) into the parametric equation for \\( x \\), we get:\n\n\\[\n4 = 2 + 3t\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( t \\):\n\n\\[\n4 - 2 = 3t \\implies 2 = 3t \\implies t = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\( t = \\frac{2}{3} \\) into the parametric equation for \\( z \\) to find the \\( z \\)-coordinate:\n\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = 1 - 2 = -1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1}\n\\]",
