      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the equation of the line passing through the points \\((2, 2, 1)\\) and \\((5, 1, -2)\\), and then determine the \\(z\\)-coordinate of a point on this line where the \\(x\\)-coordinate is 4.\n\n### Step 1: Find the direction vector of the line\nThe direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) of the line can be found by subtracting the coordinates of the first point from the coordinates of the second point:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{d} = (5 - 2, 1 - 2, -2 - 1) = (3, -1, -3)\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Use the parametric equations of the line\nThe parametric equations of the line can be written as:\n\\[\nx = 2 + 3t\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 2 - t\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3t\n\\]\nwhere \\(t\\) is a parameter.\n\n### Step 3: Determine the value of \\(t\\) when \\(x = 4\\)\nWe need to find \\(t\\) such that \\(x = 4\\). Using the parametric equation for \\(x\\):\n\\[\n4 = 2 + 3t\n\\]\nSolving for \\(t\\):\n\\[\n4 - 2 = 3t \\implies 2 = 3t \\implies t = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Find the \\(z\\)-coordinate using the value of \\(t\\)\nNow, we substitute \\(t = \\frac{2}{3}\\) into the parametric equation for \\(z\\):\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3t = 1 - 3 \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = 1 - 2 = -1\n\\]\n\nThus, the \\(z\\)-coordinate of the point on the line where the \\(x\\)-coordinate is 4 is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
